  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 314 AM EDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nRadar imagery shows a band of light rain with some embedded heavier \nshowers moving through the eastern UP early this morning. This \nactivity is ahead of a cold front currently analyzed over the \ncentral UP as of 2 AM Thursday, extending from a 1002 mb surface low \ncrossing northern Ontario. Embedded thunderstorms with abundant \nlightning strikes were observed over the western and central UP last \nevening, but the thunder threat has ended as instability wanes \novernight with eastward extent. The last of the rain should exit the \nfar eastern UP around daybreak, making for a dry, cool, and breezy \nday in the post frontal environment. High temperatures will plummet \nby 15-20 degrees compared to yesterday across most of the area as \n850 mb temps crash down from 10 to 12 C yesterday to -3 to -5 C by \nthis afternoon, corresponding to highs in the upper 40s in the west \nand the low to mid 50s elsewhere. Temps will be a bit warmer closer \nto Lake Michigan, with highs around 60 degrees expected. Cold air \nadvection and pressure rises behind the front will also create \nbreezy conditions with west-northwest winds today. Expect 25-35 mph \ngusts to be common near Lake Superior today, with 15-20 mph gusts \nexpected across the southern tier of the UP. Although most of the UP \nwill stay dry behind the front, some of the hi-res guidance does \nhint at some lake effect rain developing over the east later today \ninto tonight as colder air advects over the still relatively warm \n(10-12 C) waters of eastern Lake Superior. Will thus maintain 15-20% \nPoPs over parts of Luce and eastern Alger Counties, but any rainfall \nfrom these showers would be minimal.\n\nFriday will be a similar day across the area, but with lighter winds \nas surface high pressure continues to build in from the northern \nPlains and moves directly overhead. High temperatures will once \nagain be in the upper 40s to mid 50s across most of the area. High \npressure lingers over the Great Lakes through the weekend, yielding \na gradual moderating trend in the temperatures with highs more \nsolidly in the 50s on Saturday and perhaps approaching the low 60s \nby Sunday. A weak disturbance passing south of the area could bring \na slight chance for some showers late Saturday into early Sunday, \nbut generally expect most locations to stay dry into the weekend. \nGuidance continues to hint at a stronger system moving out of the \nPlains and approaching the Great Lakes during the early part of next \nweek as a deep upper level trough digs over the central CONUS. There \nremains a high degree of ensemble spread with regard to the precise \ndetails of track, timing and intensity of this system, lending below \naverage confidence to the forecast. Have therefore utilized a broad-\nbrushed, NBM heavy approach with chance PoPs covering the early to \nmiddle part of next week until details start to come into better \nfocus.",
